// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-10-29 (Thursday): Pure math sequence — Van Eck's sequence
// ============================================================================
//
// THE SEQUENCE (OEIS A181391): a(0) = 0. For n >= 1, look at a(n-1) — if it
// has never appeared before position n-1, a(n) = 0 ("brand new value"); if it
// HAS appeared before, a(n) = the distance back to its most recent prior
// occurrence. So the sequence is literally a record of its own memory: each
// term is either "this is new" (0) or "I last saw this exactly k steps ago"
// (k). Verified by direct computation of the defining rule (not guessed),
// terms 0-23:
//   0 0 1 0 2 0 2 2 1 6 0 5 0 2 6 5 4 0 5 3 0 3 2 9
//
// PITCH: these 24 terms, IN ORDER, are the melody's scale degrees — no mod/
// transform. Grouped 3-per-cycle across 8 cycles (+ a 1-term coda), each
// group hand-phrased with rests and held notes for breathing room (the rests
// are phrasing, not sequence data — the pitch values and their order are
// untouched). Groups:
//   C1 [0,0,1]  C2 [0,2,0]  C3 [2,2,1]  C4 [6,0,5]  C5 [0,2,6]
//   C6 [5,4,0]  C7 [5,3,0]  C8 [3,2,9]  C9 [0]  (coda)
// SCALE: B:locrian (7 degrees: B C D E F G A) — chosen for this topic because
// locrian has no stable perfect 5th, the one diatonic mode built entirely on
// tension; a sequence whose whole content is "how unstable is this value's
// memory" wants a scale with no stable center either.
//
// STRUCTURE (jazz-improv devices): C1-C2 is the CALL (low, searching, ends
// unresolved on degree 2). C3-C4 is the RESPONSE and the piece's real
// pivot — C4's 6 -> 0 is TENSION -> RELEASE made of real data: degree 6 (A)
// is the single highest "memory distance" in this run, degree 0 is the
// tonic, and the sequence itself puts them back to back. C5-C6 is
// MOTIF-AND-VARIATION, restating the climb to 6 before resolving down to the
// tonic by step. C7 recalls the descent once more, lower. C8 is the climax:
// degree 9 wraps past the 7-degree scale to degree 2 ONE OCTAVE UP — a real
// octave leap generated by the sequence's own biggest term, not inserted by
// hand. C9 is a one-note coda on the tonic: a(n)=0 always means "a fresh
// start," so the piece ends the way the sequence itself always can.
//
// NO DRUM LAYER: like Kolakoski (2026-10-22), Van Eck's sequence has no
// known period — it is a record of aperiodic recurrence, the opposite of a
// metric pulse, so a kick/hat grid would contradict the topic rather than
// serve it. Instead the one real "how far back" number this topic produces
// each cycle (that cycle's own peak nonzero term, i.e. its longest memory
// reach) drives TWO automation parameters already in the arrangement —
// filter brightness and delay time — rather than a decorative kit. Per
// cycle peak: C1=1 C2=2 C3=2 C4=6 C5=6 C6=5 C7=5 C8=9 C9=0.
//
// ARRANGEMENT (synth basics per MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5): lead is
// `sawtooth` — harmonically dense, filterable, fits the sequence's erratic
// character better than a pure tone. Drone is a dyad of the tonic (B,
// degree 0) + the tension tone (A, degree 6) on `triangle` — soft contrast
// against the sawtooth lead — entering only from C4 (the first appearance
// of 6) through C8, collapsing back to a bare tonic for the C9 coda,
// mirroring the melody's own "tension enters, then a fresh start" arc.
//
// TEMPO: setcpm(27), 4 beats/cycle = 108 BPM. CYCLES: 9.
// 27 x 9 / 60 = 20s.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(27)

$lead: n("<[0@2 ~ 1] [0 ~ 2 0] [~ 2@2 1] [6 0 5] [0 ~ 2 6] [5 4 ~ 0] [~ 5 3 0] [3 2 9] [0]>")
  .scale("B:locrian")
  .sound("sawtooth")
  .attack(.01).decay(.1).sustain(.05).release(.15)
  .lpf("<1050 1200 1200 1800 1800 1650 1650 2250 900>")
  .lpq(2)
  .delaytime("<0.08 0.11 0.11 0.23 0.23 0.20 0.20 0.32 0.05>")
  .delayfeedback(.25)
  .delay(.3)
  .gain(.4)

$drone: n("<~ ~ ~ [0,6] [0,6] [0,6] [0,6] [0,6] 0>")
  .scale("B:locrian")
  .sound("triangle")
  .attack(1).release(2)
  .lpf(500)
  .gain(.18)
  .room(2)
